  "why": "The statement asks for liminf zero for every real pair, so the classical restrictions (cubic fields, almost-every pair, Pollington–Velani, Adamczewski–Bugeaud, a.e. on lines, Chow–Technau with zero shifts) are cases, all before the snapshot. Weighted, inhomogeneous, counting, p-adic, function-field, divisibility, uniform and namesake results are variants. The uniform-conjecture disproofs fail a stronger statement and the papers themselves say the classical conjecture is untouched, so they are variants with no object role. EKL's dimension-zero exceptional set and the m_LC upper bounds move quantities whose determination would answer the problem, so they are bounds. The only in-window event on the named quantity is the unlocated Matala-Aho–Törmä–Vapa m_LC bound; the year 2026 places it after the snapshot, and I kept it as a doubtful, unreviewed bound rather than discarding it, because Wikipedia cites it in a named journal. GPT-5.6 Pro in Neckrasov–Wu–Yang is quoted for 'assist in doing calculations and checking proofs', which does not show that the model produced any mathematics in the result. That event is a variant anyway, so I answered none. Usuki 2024 (10 Jan) predates the snapshot and is a counting statement for almost all pairs, so I answered variant.",
  "doubts": "The Matala-Aho–Törmä–Vapa bound was never located. If it is not really an upper bound on m_LC, or not really from 2026, the in-window movement on this problem disappears. Calling EKL a bound rather than a case was a close call; it is before the snapshot either way. Journal acceptances for the pre-2024 papers come from Wikipedia and were not read at source.",
